at approach fails because the only natural number solution for k would be 1, but 1^1995=1\u22602. So linear functions won't work.\n\nAlternatively, maybe f is an affine function, like f(n) = an + b. But composing affine functions multiple times can get complicated. Let's test with a simple case. Suppose f(n) = n + c. Then f^(1995)(n) = n + 1995c. Setting this equal to 2n would require 1995c = n, which can't hold for all n. So affine functions with addition also don't work.\n\nHmm, maybe linear functions are too restrictive. Let's think differently. Perhaps f acts differently on different parts of the natural numbers. For example, partitioning N into two sets where f behaves differently on each set. But how?\n\nWait, another idea: if we can decompose the natural numbers into chains where each chain has length 1995, such that moving 1995 steps along the chain doubles the starting number. For example, for each n, define a sequence n, f(n), f(f(n)), ..., f^(1995)(n) = 2n. Then, each number in the chain must be part of a progression leading to 2n. But since we need this for every natural number n, we have to cover all natural numbers in such chains without overlap.\n\nBut how can we partition N into such chains? Each chain would start at some number m and progress m \u2192 a1 \u2192 a2 \u2192 ... \u2192 a1995 = 2m. However, the problem is that 2m must be the end of the chain starting at m. Then, 2m must also start its own chain leading to 4m, and so on. But in this case, numbers that are not powers of 2 might be left out. Wait, no, because every number can be written as 2^k * odd. Maybe using the structure of the natural numbers based on their prime factors.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each number is part of an infinite chain where each step is defined by f, and after 1995 steps, it's doubled. But how to handle numbers that are not in the form 2^k? For example, take n=3. Then f^(1995)(3) must be 6. So 3 must be part of a chain leading to 6 after 1995 steps, 6 must le