inA sinB cosA cosB + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nThen, S = cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + [sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 sinA sinB cosA cosB + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B] + 2 cosA cosB [sinA sinB - cosA cosB]\n\nLet me expand this step by step.\n\nFirst, expand cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B:\n\n= cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B\n\nThen, add cos\u00b2C:\n\n= cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 sinA sinB cosA cosB + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nNow, add the term 2 cosA cosB cosC. Since cosC = sinA sinB - cosA cosB, this becomes:\n\n2 cosA cosB (sinA sinB - cosA cosB) = 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nTherefore, adding this to the previous expression:\n\nTotal S = [cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 sinA sinB cosA cosB + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B] + [2 cosA cosB sinA sinB - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B]\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\nThe -2 sinA sinB cosA cosB and +2 sinA sinB cosA cosB cancel each other.\n\nSimilarly, cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B = -cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nSo now, S = cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nHmm, this is getting messy. Maybe another approach. Let's use the identity sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = 2 + 2 cosA cosB cosC, which is the first identity. Wait, but if we can relate this to the second identity, we can connect them. Since earlier substitution showed that sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = 3 - (cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C), so setting this equal to 2(1 + cosA cosB cosC):\n\n3 - (cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) = 2 + 2 cosA cosB cosC\n\nTherefore, rearranged:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 3 - 2 - 2 cosA cosB cosC = 1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC\n\nWhich is exactly the second identity. So if we can prove the first identity, the second follows. Alternatively, if we can prove the second identity, the first follows. So maybe we need to find a way to prove one of them from scratch.\n\nAlternatively, use vector algebra. Since in a triangle, the angles are related to the vectors of the sides. Wait, maybe not necessary. Let me think again.\n\nAnother approach: use the formula for cosine of angles in a triangle. Let\u2019s recall the law of cosines:\n\ncosA = (b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - a\u00b2)/(2bc)\n\nSimilarly for cosB and cosC. But this might complicate things with the sides. Maybe not the best approach.\n\nAlternatively, use sum-to-product formulas. Let me consider that A + B + C = \u03c0, so C = \u03c0 - A - B. Let's express everything in terms of A and B.\n\nExpress cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C. Since C = \u03c0 - A - B, so cosC = -cos(A + B). Then,\n\ncos\u00b2C = cos\u00b2(A + B) = [cos(A+B)]\u00b2 = [cosA cosB - sinA sinB]^2 = cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B\n\nSo, cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B\n\nLet me group terms:\n\n= cos\u00b2A (1 + cos\u00b2B) + cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB\n\nHmm, not sure. Maybe factor differently. Let's collect terms with sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B:\n\n= cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB\n\nNotice that the last three terms: cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB. This resembles (cosA cosB - sinA sinB)^2, which is [cos(A + B)]\u00b2. But cos(A + B) = -cosC, so [cos(A + B)]\u00b2 = cos\u00b2C. Wait, but we already have cos\u00b2C in the expression. Hmm, maybe this isn't helpful.\n\nAlternatively, notice that sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B can be written as (1 - cos\u00b2A)(1 - cos\u00b2B) = 1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B.\n\nSo replacing sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B with that:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B + [1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B] - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B + 1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB\n\nThe cos\u00b2A and -cos\u00b2A cancel, same with cos\u00b2B and -cos\u00b2B. So we have:\n\n1 + 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB\n\nFactor out 2 cosA cosB:\n\n= 1 + 2 cosA cosB (cosA cosB - sinA sinB)\n\nBut cosA cosB - sinA sinB = cos(A + B) = cos(\u03c0 - C) = -cosC. So:\n\n= 1 + 2 cosA cosB (-cosC)\n\n= 1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC\n\nTherefore, we have shown that cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC, which is the second identity. Hence, proven.\n\nSince we already saw that the first identity is a consequence of the second one, substituting back:\n\nsin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = 3 - (cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) = 3 - (1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC) = 2 + 2 cosA cosB cosC = 2(1 + cosA cosB cosC)\n\nTherefore, both identities are proven.\n\nBut let me double-check the steps to ensure there's no mistake. Especially in expanding the terms when substituting sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B. Let's recap that part.\n\nStarting from:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C\n\nExpressed as:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + [cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B]\n\nThen, when adding the 2 cosA cosB cosC term (for the standard identity), which becomes:\n\n2 cosA cosB (sinA sinB - cosA cosB) = 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nAdding this to the previous expression:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B + 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nSimplify:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + [cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B + 2 cosA cosB sinA sinB - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B]\n\nThe -2 cosA cosB sinA sinB and +2 cosA cosB sinA sinB cancel.\n\ncos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - 2 cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B = -cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nThen, the remaining terms:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B - cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nThen, replacing sin\u00b2A sin\u00b2B with (1 - cos\u00b2A)(1 - cos\u00b2B) = 1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + [1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B] - cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nSimplify:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + 1 - cos\u00b2A - cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B - cos\u00b2A cos\u00b2B\n\nAll terms cancel except 1. Wait, but earlier we had an extra term from the standard identity?\n\nWait, perhaps there was a miscalculation here. Wait, let's re-examine.\n\nWait, when we considered S = cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C + 2 cosA cosB cosC, we expanded cos\u00b2C and then added 2 cosA cosB cosC, which led us through the steps to eventually get S = 1. But perhaps a more straightforward way is needed.\n\nAlternatively, let's use complex numbers or Euler's formula? Hmm, maybe overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, use the identity for the sum of cos squares in a triangle. Wait, actually, there is a well-known identity in triangle trigonometry:\n\nIn any triangle, cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C + 2 cosA cosB cosC = 1\n\nTherefore, rearranged, we get the second identity. So if this is a known identity, then we can cite it. However, since the problem asks to prove it, we need to show the steps.\n\nAlternatively, another approach: using the formula for the product cosA cosB cosC.\n\nWait, in a triangle, there's a relation involving the product of cosines. Let me recall that in a triangle:\n\ncosA + cosB + cosC = 1 + r/R\n\nWhere r is the inradius and R is the circumradius. Not sure if that helps here.\n\nAlternatively, use the identity:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = -1 - 4 cos(A + B) cos(A + C) cos(B + C)\n\nBut since A + B + C = \u03c0, then A + B = \u03c0 - C, etc. So cos(A + B) = cos(\u03c0 - C) = -cosC. Therefore:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = -1 - 4 (-cosC)(-cosB)(-cosA) = -1 - 4 (-cosA cosB cosC) = -1 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nBut cos2\u03b8 = 2cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 1. Therefore:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = 2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) - 3\n\nSetting equal to the previous expression:\n\n2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) - 3 = -1 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nRearranging:\n\n2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) = 2 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 1 + 2 cosA cosB cosC\n\nWait, but this contradicts the identity we wanted to prove. Wait, no, actually, according to this, it would be cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 1 + 2 cosA cosB cosC, but the problem states it's equal to 1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC. So there's a discrepancy here. That means I must have made a mistake in the steps.\n\nWait, let's check the identity:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = ?\n\nIn a triangle where A + B + C = \u03c0.\n\nLet me compute cos2A + cos2B + cos2C.\n\nExpressing 2A, 2B, 2C. Since A + B + C = \u03c0, then 2A + 2B + 2C = 2\u03c0. So, angles 2A, 2B, 2C are angles on a circle, but not necessarily forming a triangle. Let me use the identity for sum of cosines of three angles adding up to 2\u03c0.\n\nAlternatively, use complex exponentials. Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, use the formula:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = -1 - 4 cos(A + B) cos(A + C) cos(B + C)\n\nBut as A + B = \u03c0 - C, so cos(A + B) = -cosC. Similarly for others.\n\nTherefore:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = -1 - 4 (-cosC)(-cosB)(-cosA) = -1 - 4 (-cosA cosB cosC) = -1 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nBut on the other hand, cos2A + cos2B + cos2C = 2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) - 3\n\nTherefore:\n\n2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) - 3 = -1 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nThen, moving -3 to the right:\n\n2(cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C) = 2 + 4 cosA cosB cosC\n\nDivide by 2:\n\ncos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 1 + 2 cosA cosB cosC\n\nBut this conflicts with the identity we need to prove, which is cos\u00b2A + cos\u00b2B + cos\u00b2C = 1 - 2 cosA cosB cosC. Hence, there must be an error in the steps. Wait, but where?\n\nWait, perhaps the formula for the sum of cos2A in a triangle is different. Let me verify this