rilateral**: Points \\(B, C, P, Q\\) lie on circle \\(k\\), making \\(BCPQ\\) a cyclic quadrilateral. This implies that angles subtended by the same arc are equal.\n\n4. **Angle at the Center**: The angle \\(\\angle PA_1Q\\) is formed by the secants \\(A_1P\\) and \\(A_1Q\\) intersecting outside the circle. According to the theorem, the measure of the angle formed by two secants intersecting outside the circle is half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n5. ** intercepted Arcs**: The angle \\(\\angle PA_1Q\\) is half the difference of the arcs intercepted by the secants. Since \\(A_1\\) is equidistant from \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\), and considering the symmetry and properties of the cyclic quadrilateral, the difference of the intercepted arcs corresponds to twice the angle \\(\\angle BAC\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**: By the properties of cyclic quadrilaterals and the theorem on angles formed by secants, we conclude that \\(\\angle PA_1Q = 2\\angle BAC\\).\n\nThus, the angle \\(\\angle PA_1Q\\) is twice the angle \\(\\angle BAC\\), leading to the final answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\angle PA_1Q = 2\\angle A}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Step 1: Understand the problem layout**  \n    We are given a triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(AB > AC\\). Circle \\(k\\) passes through vertices \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) and intersects the extensions of sides \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) at points \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) respectively. We are to prove that the angle \\(PA_1Q\\) is twice the angle \\(A\\) of the triangle where \\(A_1\\) is the foot of the altitude from \\(A\\) to \\(BC\\) and \\(A_1P = A_1Q\\).\n\n2. **Step 2: Analyze the circle and triangle relationships**  \n    Since \\(A_1P = A_1Q\\), \\(A_1\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(PQ\\). Note also that \\(AA_1\\) is perpendicular to \\(BC\\), thus \\(\\angle A_1AP = \\angle A_1AQ = 90^\\circ - \\angle BAC\\) because the triangles share the base angles at \\(A\\).\n\n3. **Step 3: Identify the circumcenter \\(O\\)** 