nFor s=2:\n\nk=182 +10*t, t=0 to18 \u21d2 k=182,192,...,182+180=362.\n\nBut since t can be 0-18, that's 19 values.\n\nSimilarly for s=3:\n\nk=273 +10*t, t=0-18 \u21d2273,283,...,273+180=453. 19 values.\n\nSimilarly up to s=9:\n\nk=819 +10*t, t=0-18 \u21d2819,829,...,819+180=999.\n\nWait, 819 +10*18=819+180=999. Which is \u2264909? No, 999 is greater than909. Wait, s=9:\n\n91*9=819. Then k=819 +10*t. We need k \u2264909. So 819 +10*t \u2264909 \u219210*t \u226490 \u2192t \u22649. So for s=9, t can be 0-9, giving k=819,829,...,819+90=909.\n\nTherefore, for s=9, t ranges from0 to9 (10 values).\n\nSimilarly, for s=8:\n\n91*8=728. Then k=728 +10*t \u2264909 \u219210*t \u2264181 \u2192t \u226418.1. But t can only go up to18. So for s=8, t=0-18 (19 values), and k up to728+180=908.\n\nSimilarly for s=7:\n\n91*7=637. k=637 +10*t \u2264909 \u219210*t \u2264272 \u2192t \u226427.2. But t is limited to18, so t=0-18 (19 values). So k=637,...,637+180=817.\n\nSimilarly for s=6:91*6=546. k=546+10*t. 546+180=726 \u2264909, so t=0-18.\n\nSimilarly s=5:91*5=455. k=455+10*t. 455+180=635 \u2264909.\n\ns=4:364+10*t. 364+180=544 \u2264909.\n\ns=3:273+10*t. 273+180=453 \u2264909.\n\ns=2:182+10*t. 182+180=362 \u2264909.\n\nSo for s=2 to8, t=0-18 (19 values each), and for s=9, t=0-9 (10 values).\n\nNow, the problem is overlapping ranges. For example, s=2, t=0 gives k=182, which is unique. s=2, t=10 gives k=282. Similarly, s=3, t=0 gives k=273, which is between s=2's k=182 and 362. So there might be overlaps between different s values.\n\nTherefore, we can't simply sum the number of k's for each s, because there might be overlaps where the same k is generated by different (s,t) pairs. So we need to count the distinct k's.\n\nBut how?\n\nThis seems complicated. Maybe there's another approach.\n\nSince 91 and10 are coprime, as gcd(91,10)=1, by the Coin Problem, every integer \u2265(91*10 -91 -10)=809 can be expressed as 91*s +10*t for non-negative integers s,t. But in our case, s starts at2 (s\u