riangle, cos2A + cos2B + cos2C + 2cosA cosB cosC = 1. So our given condition is cos2A + cos2B + cos2C = 1, which implies that 2cosA cosB cosC = 0. Therefore, cosA cosB cosC = 0. So one of the angles has cosine zero, i.e., one angle is \u03c0/2. Therefore, the triangle is right-angled.\n\nWait, hold on. If cosA cosB cosC = 0, then one of the angles is right angle. So triangle ABC is right-angled. So the given condition sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C =1 is equivalent to the triangle being right-angled. Let me verify this.\n\nIn a right-angled triangle, say angle C is \u03c0/2. Then, sinC = 1. Then sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + 1. But in a right-angled triangle, A + B = \u03c0/2. So sinB = cosA, sinA = cosB. Then sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B = cos\u00b2B + sin\u00b2B = 1. Therefore, sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = 1 + 1 = 2. Wait, that contradicts the given condition. Wait, so perhaps I made an error here.\n\nWait, in the problem statement, the given condition is sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C =1, but in a right-angled triangle, sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C = sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + 1. If angles A and B are complementary, then sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B = sin\u00b2A + cos\u00b2A =1. So indeed, sin\u00b2A + sin\u00b2B + sin\u00b2C =1 +1=2. So the sum is 2, not 1. So that contradicts. Therefore, my previous conclusion that the triangle is right-angled is wrong. Hmm, so maybe there's a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let's check the identity again. The standard identity for a triangle is:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = -1 - 4cosA cosB cosC\n\nWait, maybe I need to check this again. Let's recall that in any triangle:\n\nA + B + C = \u03c0\n\nSo let me compute cos2A + cos2B + cos2C.\n\nUsing the identity for sum of cosines of angles in a triangle.\n\nAlternatively, use the formula:\n\ncos2A + cos2B + cos2C = 1 - 2sin\u00b2A + 1 - 2sin\u00b2B + 1 - 2sin\u00b2C\n\nWait, no. Wait, cos2\u03b8 = 1 - 2sin\u00b2\u03b8. Therefor