n\n1. (sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2, sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2 )\n\n2. (-sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2, sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2 )\n\n3. (-sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2, -sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2 )\n\n4. (sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2, -sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2 )\n\nBut let me check the signs again.\n\nWait, for \u03b8 = 3\u03c0/8: first quadrant, so both x and y positive.\n\n\u03b8 = 7\u03c0/8: second quadrant, x negative, y positive.\n\n\u03b8 = 11\u03c0/8: third quadrant, x and y negative.\n\n\u03b8 = 15\u03c0/8: fourth quadrant, x positive, y negative.\n\nSo, that makes sense.\n\nTherefore, these four points on the unit circle satisfy the second equation.\n\nBut let me verify if these actually satisfy the second equation \\( 4xy(2y^2 -1) =1 \\). Because sometimes when we use substitution, especially trigonometric, sometimes extraneous solutions can be introduced, although in this case, since we parametrized all solutions on the unit circle, and found \u03b8 such that the second equation holds, they should be valid. But let me check with one of them.\n\nTake the first solution: x = sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2, y = sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2\n\nCompute 4xy(2y\u00b2 -1):\n\nFirst, compute x and y:\n\nsqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2 \u2248 sqrt(2 - 1.4142)/2 \u2248 sqrt(0.5858)/2 \u2248 0.7654/2 \u2248 0.3827\n\nSimilarly, y \u2248 sqrt(2 + 1.4142)/2 \u2248 sqrt(3.4142)/2 \u2248 1.8478/2 \u2248 0.9239\n\nNow compute 2y\u00b2 -1:\n\ny\u00b2 = (sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2)^2 = (2 + sqrt(2))/4\n\nSo 2y\u00b2 -1 = (2 + sqrt(2))/2 -1 = (2 + sqrt(2) - 2)/2 = sqrt(2)/2\n\nThen, 4xy(2y\u00b2 -1) = 4 * x * y * sqrt(2)/2 = 4 * x * y * sqrt(2)/2 = 2 * x * y * sqrt(2)\n\nCompute x * y:\n\nx * y = [sqrt(2 - sqrt(2))/2] * [sqrt(2 + sqrt(2))/2] = [sqrt{(2 - sqrt(2))(2 + sqrt(2))}]/4\n\nMultiply inside the sqrt: (2)^2 - (sqrt(2))^2 = 4 - 2 = 2. So sqrt(2)/4\n\nThus, x * y = sqrt(2)/4\n\nTherefore, 2 * x * y * sqrt(2) = 2 * (sqrt(2)/4) * sqrt(2) = 2 * (2/4) = 2*(1/2) = 1. Perfect, that works.\n\nSimilarly, let's check another solution. Take the second solution: x = -sqrt(2 + 