{14} = a_2\\), and so on.\n\n3. Each individual number \\(a_i\\) appears exactly in four sums (two adjacent and two with two vertices in between). For \\(a_i + a_{i+1}\\) to be prime and \\(a_i + a_{i+3}\\) to be prime, appropriate pairs need to be checked for prime conditions.\n\n4. Consider the numbers 6 and 12 specifically. A number that forms a prime when added to either 6 or 12 has to be one of \\(1, 5, 7\\), or \\(11\\):\n   - If \\(a_i = 6\\), then for \\(a_i + a_{i+1}\\) and \\(a_i + a_{i-1}\\) to be prime: \\(a_{i+1} \\in \\{1, 5, 7, 11\\}\\) and \\(a_{i-1} \\in \\{1, 5, 7, 11\\}\\).\n   - Similarly, if \\(a_i = 12\\), \\(a_{i+1} \\in \\{1, 5, 7, 11\\}\\) and \\(a_{i-1} \\in \\{1, 5, 7, 11\\}\\).\n\n5. However, there are fewer numbers \\(1, 5, 7,\\) and \\(11\\) than required to satisfy all conditions for both 6 and 12 simultaneously, also considering sums involving pairs with two numbers between them, it adds complexity to satisfy all prime conditions.\n\n6. Given that every number from 1 to 12 must participate in being the component of four sums and must match the strict prime condition for those, there's an additional conflict created due to the limited possible primes and the cyclic nature of vertices.\n\n### Conclusion\nHence, it is not possible for all sums of both adjacent and the pairs of vertices which have exactly two vertices in-between them to simultaneously be prime numbers. At least one of these sums will fail the prime condition.\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\text{not possible}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "By how much is the center of the upper base of a cube with an edge length of 1 further from the vertex of the lower base than from its side?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out by how much the center of the upper base of a cube with edge length 1 is further from a vertex of the lower base compared to its distance from a side of the lower base. Hmm, let me visua