iangle ABC, the altitude from A is the line AH, which is perpendicular to BC. Similarly, the altitude from B is BH, perpendicular to AC, and from C is CH, perpendicular to AB.\n\nSince O is the circumcenter, OA = OB = OC. Let me consider triangle AOB. Since OA = OB, triangle AOB is isoceles, so angles at A and B are equal. Wait, angles OAB and OBA are equal. So angle OAB = angle OBA.\n\nSimilarly, in triangle AOC, OA = OC, so angles OAC and OCA are equal.\n\nWait, angle OAB is angle BAO, and angle OAC is angle CAO. So angle BAO = angle OBA, and angle CAO = angle OCA. Hmm.\n\nBut we need angle CAH. Let's see, angle CAH is the angle between CA and AH. Since AH is the altitude from A to BC, which is perpendicular to BC. So angle CAH is part of the right angle at H. Wait, no. The altitude AH is from A to BC, so AH is perpendicular to BC. Therefore, angle CAH is complementary to angle ACB.\n\nWait, let me clarify. In triangle ABC, since AH is perpendicular to BC, angle AHB is 90 degrees. Wait, no, angle AHC is 90 degrees if H is on BC? Wait, no. The altitude from A is the line AH, which meets BC at the foot of the perpendicular, say D. Then angle ADH is 90 degrees.\n\nWait, perhaps better to use coordinate geometry. Let me assign coordinates to the triangle to compute the angles.\n\nLet me place triangle ABC such that BC is horizontal for simplicity. Let me set point B at (0, 0), point C at (c, 0), and point A at (a, b). Then, the circumcenter O is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides. The orthocenter H is the intersection of the altitudes.\n\nFirst, let's compute O. The perpendicular bisector of AB: midpoint of AB is ((a/2), (b/2)), and the slope of AB is (b - 0)/(a - 0) = b/a. Therefore, the perpendicular bisector has slope -a/b. Similarly, the perpendicular bisector of AC: midpoint of AC is ((a + c)/2, b/2), and slope of AC is (b - 0)/(a - c) = b/(a - c). Therefore, the perpendicular bisector has slope (c - a)/b.\n\nSo the perpendicular bisecto