roceed with that approach, even though the integrals end up being the same as using f(x).\n\nSo, first, confirm that g(y) is correct:\n\nGiven Y = X\u00b2, and X has density f(x) = (1/2) sin(x) on (0, \u03c0). Since X is in (0, \u03c0), Y is in (0, \u03c0\u00b2). The function Y = X\u00b2 is monotonically increasing on (0, \u03c0), so we can use the transformation technique:\n\ng(y) = f(x) * |dx/dy|, where x = sqrt(y).\n\nSince dx/dy = 1/(2 sqrt(y)), then:\n\ng(y) = (1/2) sin(sqrt(y)) * (1/(2 sqrt(y))) ) = (1/(4 sqrt(y))) sin(sqrt(y)) for 0 < y < \u03c0\u00b2.\n\nThat's correct.\n\nNow, compute E[Y] = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0\u00b2} y * g(y) dy = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0\u00b2} y * [1/(4 sqrt(y)) sin(sqrt(y))] dy\n\nSimplify the integrand:\n\ny / (4 sqrt(y)) = sqrt(y)/4, so E[Y] = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0\u00b2} (sqrt(y)/4) sin(sqrt(y)) dy\n\nLet\u2019s perform substitution: let t = sqrt(y), so y = t\u00b2, dy = 2t dt. When y=0, t=0; y=\u03c0\u00b2, t=\u03c0.\n\nThus, E[Y] becomes \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t/4) sin(t) * 2t dt = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t/4) * 2t sin(t) dt = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t\u00b2/2) sin(t) dt\n\nWhich is the same as \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (1/2) t\u00b2 sin(t) dt. Which matches the expectation E[X\u00b2] using f(x), since f(x) = (1/2) sin(x), so E[X\u00b2] = \u222b x\u00b2 (1/2) sin(x) dx from 0 to \u03c0. So yes, it's the same integral.\n\nSimilarly, E[Y\u00b2] = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0\u00b2} y\u00b2 g(y) dy = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0\u00b2} y\u00b2 * [1/(4 sqrt(y)) sin(sqrt(y))] dy\n\nSimplify y\u00b2 / (4 sqrt(y)) = y^(3/2)/4 = (y^(3/2))/4. Let t = sqrt(y), so y = t\u00b2, dy = 2t dt. Then:\n\nE[Y\u00b2] = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t\u00b2)^(3/2)/4 * sin(t) * 2t dt = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t\u00b3)/4 * sin(t) * 2t dt\n\nWait, (t\u00b2)^(3/2) is t\u00b3, so y^(3/2) = t\u00b3. Then:\n\nE[Y\u00b2] = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t\u00b3 /4) * sin(t) * 2t dt = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (2t\u2074 /4) sin(t) dt = \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} (t\u2074 /2) sin(t) dt\n\nAgain, this is the same as E[X\u2074] via f(x), since \u222b x\u2074 (1/2) sin(x) dx.\n\nSo, regardless of the approach, we need to compute these two integrals: \u222b x\u00b2 sin(x) dx from 0 to \u03c0, and \u222b x\u2074 sin(x) dx from 0 to \u03c0, each multiplied by 1/2.\n\nTherefore, the variance Var(Y) = E[Y\u00b2] - (E[Y])\u00b2 = (1/2 \u222b x\u2074 sin(x) dx) - (1/2 \u222b x\u00b2 sin(x) dx)^2.\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals. I'll need to compute \u222b x\u00b2 sin(x) dx and \u222b x\u2074 sin(x) dx from 0 to \u03c0.\n\nFirst, compute E[Y] = 1/2 \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} x\u00b2 sin(x) dx\n\nTo integrate x\u00b2 sin(x), we can use integration by parts. Let me recall that \u222b x^n sin(x) dx can be handled by parts, where we let u = x^n and dv = sin(x) dx. Then du = n x^(n-1) dx, and v = -cos(x).\n\nFor \u222b x\u00b2 sin(x) dx:\n\nLet u = x\u00b2 \u21d2 du = 2x dx\n\ndv = sin(x) dx \u21d2 v = -cos(x)\n\nIntegration by parts gives:\n\nuv - \u222b v du = -x\u00b2 cos(x) + \u222b 2x cos(x) dx\n\nNow, compute \u222b 2x cos(x) dx. Again, use integration by parts:\n\nu = 2x \u21d2 du = 2 dx\n\ndv = cos(x) dx \u21d2 v = sin(x)\n\nThus:\n\n2x sin(x) - \u222b 2 sin(x) dx = 2x sin(x) + 2 cos(x)\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\u222b x\u00b2 sin(x) dx = -x\u00b2 cos(x) + 2x sin(x) + 2 cos(x) + C\n\nEvaluate from 0 to \u03c0:\n\nAt \u03c0:\n\n-\u03c0\u00b2 cos(\u03c0) + 2\u03c0 sin(\u03c0) + 2 cos(\u03c0) = -\u03c0\u00b2 (-1) + 0 + 2 (-1) = \u03c0\u00b2 - 2\n\nAt 0:\n\n-0\u00b2 cos(0) + 0 + 2 cos(0) = 0 + 0 + 2(1) = 2\n\nSubtracting lower limit from upper limit:\n\n(\u03c0\u00b2 - 2) - 2 = \u03c0\u00b2 - 4\n\nThus, \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} x\u00b2 sin(x) dx = \u03c0\u00b2 - 4\n\nTherefore, E[Y] = (1/2)(\u03c0\u00b2 - 4)\n\nNext, compute E[Y\u00b2] = 1/2 \u222b_{0}^{\u03c0} x\u2074 sin(x) dx\n\nThis integral is more complex. Let's compute \u222b x\u2074 sin(x) dx using integration by parts multiple times.\n\nLet u = x\u2074 \u21d2 du = 4x\u00b3 dx\n\ndv = sin(x) dx \u21d2 v = -cos(x)\n\nFirst integration by parts:\n\n- x\u20