elta_n/k]. If we can choose k and n such that [x_n/k - delta_n/k, x_n/k + delta_n/k] intersects (a, b). \n\nFor large n, x_n is large. Let's set k = floor(x_n / b). Then k \u2248 x_n / b, so x_n / k \u2248 b. Therefore, alpha \u2248 b. The interval [x_n/k - delta_n/k, x_n/k + delta_n/k] is approximately [b - delta_n/k, b + delta_n/k]. Since delta_n can be small, but k \u2248 x_n / b, delta_n/k \u2248 delta_n b / x_n. If delta_n is fixed, then as x_n increases, delta_n/k becomes small. Therefore, the interval around b becomes [b - small, b + small]. Hence, if we choose n large enough such that this interval lies within (a, b), then alpha = x_n / k would be in (a, b). \n\nTherefore, for sufficiently large n, the interval [x_n/k - delta_n/k, x_n/k + delta_n/k] is contained within (a, b), implying that A_m is dense. Hence, by Baire Category Theorem, there exists an alpha in the intersection, contradicting the original assumption. \n\nThis line of reasoning seems promising. Let me try to formalize it.\n\nBy the Baire Category Theorem, in a complete metric space (like the real numbers with the usual metric), the intersection of countably many open dense sets is dense. \n\nDefine, for each m \u2208 \u2115, the set A_m = {alpha > 0 | \u2203k \u2265 m s.t. |f(k alpha)| \u2265 \u03b5/2}. Each A_m is open because f is continuous, hence for each k, the set {alpha | |f(k alpha)| \u2265 \u03b5/2} is open, and A_m is the union over k \u2265 m of these open sets. \n\nTo apply Baire, we need each A_m to be dense in (0, \u221e). To show density, take any interval (a, b) with 0 < a < b. We need to find alpha \u2208 (a, b) and k \u2265 m such that |f(k alpha)| \u2265 \u03b5/2. \n\nSince x_n \u2192 \u221e, we can choose n large enough so that x_n > m b. Then, set k = floor(x_n / a). Since x_n > m b and a < b, k = floor(x_n / a) \u2265 floor(m b / a) \u2265 m (since b/a > 1). \n\nLet alpha = x_n / k. Then, since k \u2264 x_n / a < k + 1, we have alpha = x_n / k \u2208 [a, x_n / (k)). But